\n\
I need to decide how to block. Let me think about combat math:\n\
\n\
1. If I don't block: They deal 3 damage total (1+2). I go to 15 life.\n\
\n\
2. If I block Gene Pollinator (1/2) with my Hired Claw (1/2): Both die, trade. Spider Manifestation deals 2 damage, I go to 16 life.\n\
\n\
3. If I block Spider Manifestation (2/2) with my Hired Claw (1/2): My creature dies, theirs survives with 1 damage marked (becomes 2/1 until end of turn). Gene Pollinator deals 1 damage, I go to 17 life.\n\
\n\
Option 2 seems best - trade 1/2 for 1/2, take 2 damage. I lose a creature but they lose one too, and I take less damage.\n\
\n\
Actually, wait. Spider Manifestation is a 2/2. If I don't block it, it deals 2 damage. If I block it with my 1/2, it deals 2 damage to my creature (killing it) and my creature deals 1 damage to it (marking 1 damage). So it becomes a 2/1 until end of turn. Then Gene Pollinator deals 1 damage. Total: 1 damage from Gene Pollinator, I go to 17 life. And they have a damaged 2/1 creature.\n\
\n\
If I block Gene Pollinator instead: both die (trade). Spider Manifestation deals 2 damage, I go to 16 life.\n\
\n\
So blocking Spider Manifestation results in: I take 1 damage, they have a damaged 2/1 creature. Blocking Gene Pollinator results in: I take 2 damage, both creatures die.\n\
\n\
Hmm. Taking 1 less damage is good, but leaving them with a damaged creature might be worse than trading. Let me think about next turn.\n\
\n\
