- They have a 2/1 Spider Manifestation (damaged)\n\
- I have 0 creatures (both my Hired Claws would be gone - one attacking, one blocking)\n\
- I take 1 damage\n\
\n\
If I block Gene Pollinator:\n\
- They have a 2/2 Spider Manifestation\n\
- I have 0 creatures\n\
- I take 2 damage\n\
\n\
Actually, if I block Spider Manifestation with my Hired Claw, and my attacking Hired Claw is unblocked (since I only attacked with one), then I have 0 creatures after combat (attacker might die if they block? Wait no, they didn't block it during declare blockers step on my turn. So my attacking Hired Claw should deal damage and survive).\n\
\n\
Let me re-examine: On my turn, I attacked with p2 (Hired Claw). They didn't block it (they only have tapped creatures that can't block). So my attacking Hired Claw should deal 1 damage and survive.\n\
\n\
So after combat:\n\
- If I block Spider Manifestation: I have 1 Hired Claw (the attacker), they have 2/1 Spider Manifestation (damaged)\n\
- If I block Gene Pollinator: I have 1 Hired Claw (the attacker), they have 2/2 Spider Manifestation\n\
- If I don't block: I have 1 Hired Claw (the attacker), they have both creatures\n\
\n\
Blocking Spider Manifestation seems best - I trade my blocker for damaging their 2/2 to 2/1, and keep my attacker.\n\
\n\
Actually wait, if I block Spider Manifestation with my 1/2, both creatures deal damage simultaneously. My 1/2 deals 1 damage to Spider Manifestation. Spider Manifestation deals 2 damage to my 1/2. My creature dies, theirs becomes a 2/1 (with 1 damage marked). That's not \"damaged to 2/1\" - it's still a 2/2 with 1 damage marked. At end of turn, damage wears off, it's back to 2/2.\n\
\n\
So blocking Spider Manifestation: My blocker dies, their Spider Manifestation survives unharmed after end of turn. Not good.\n\
\n\
Blocking Gene Pollinator: Both creatures die (trade). Better.\n\
\n\
I think blocking Gene Pollinator is the correct play. Trade 1/2 for 1/2, they lose a creature.\n\
